steps.body:
A backtest over ten symbols, or a risk run that reprices four books, splits cleanly: each piece reads shared input and writes its own result. Give each piece a thread, and the pieces run on separate cores at the same time.

You've started threads already: *Networking: Sockets, TCP, UDP, and Market Data Feeds* ran one `std::thread` from `<thread>`, and *Cores, Threads, and the Scheduler* kept several in a vector. A thread runs its callable from the moment it's built, and `join()` waits for it. Join every thread before its `std::thread` is destroyed, or the destructor calls `std::terminate`, which aborts the program:

```cpp error
#include <thread>

int main() {
    std::thread worker([] {});
    return 0;  // worker was never joined
}
```

Avoid `detach()`, which lets a thread run on unwatched: nothing can wait for it, and one still running when `main` returns uses objects that are being destroyed. C++20's `std::jthread`, from *What C++20 and C++23 Add*, joins itself in its destructor.

Copy small values into the lambda, like a slot index `[i]`, and capture big shared input by reference, like `[&prices]`. That's safe while every thread only reads it and it outlives the threads. Arguments after the callable are copied into the thread, so wrap a variable in `std::ref` from `<functional>` to pass it to a reference parameter:

```cpp
void add_fills(long long& total, int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        total += 100;
    }
}

long long total = 0;
std::thread t(add_fills, std::ref(total), 5);
t.join();
std::cout << total << '\n';  // 500
```

A thread can be moved but not copied, which is why a vector of them grows with `emplace_back`. Print only after `join()`, because output from running threads interleaves in an order that changes every run.

example.code:
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    int k = 0;
    int n = 0;
    std::cin >> k >> n;
    std::vector<int64_t> qty(n);
    for (int64_t& q : qty) {
        std::cin >> q;
    }

    std::vector<int64_t> partial(k, 0);  // one slot per thread
    std::vector<std::thread> workers;
    for (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) {
        workers.emplace_back([&qty, &partial, i, k, n] {
            const int begin = n * i / k;
            const int end = n * (i + 1) / k;
            for (int j = begin; j < end; ++j) {
                partial[i] += qty[j];
            }
        });
    }
    for (std::thread& t : workers) {
        t.join();
    }

    int64_t total = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) {
        std::cout << "thread " << i << ": " << partial[i] << '\n';
        total += partial[i];
    }
    std::cout << "total " << total << '\n';
    return 0;
}

