steps.body:
Every strategy thread reports its fills into one risk total that the limit check reads. If two threads run `filled += qty` at the same moment, fills get lost and the check sees less exposure than you have.

The add isn't one step. At the sandbox's default `-O0`, g++ 10.2 turns `++filled` into a load, an add, and a store:

```text
add_fill:
        push    rbp
        mov     rbp, rsp
        mov     rax, QWORD PTR filled[rip]
        add     rax, 1
        mov     QWORD PTR filled[rip], rax
        nop
        pop     rbp
        ret
```

A thread can be preempted between any two of them, as *Cores, Threads, and the Scheduler* showed, or run beside another on a second core, so two threads can both load 500 and both store 501. At `-O2` it becomes one `add QWORD PTR filled[rip], 1`, still without the `lock` prefix that would make it atomic. Two threads using one object without synchronization, at least one of them writing, is a **data race**: undefined behavior. Here, four threads each adding 1 a million times ended between 1 and 1.3 million, different on every run. ThreadSanitizer, from *Undefined Behavior, Signed and Unsigned*, catches races like this one: build with `-fsanitize=thread` in your own terminal, and it reports each race with both lines.

A `std::mutex` from `<mutex>` lets one thread at a time through. `std::lock_guard` locks it in its constructor and unlocks it in its destructor, so every exit from the scope unlocks:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    std::mutex m;
    int64_t filled = 0;
    const int64_t qty = 300;
    {{code}}
    std::cout << filled << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m);
filled += qty;
```

Locking a free mutex isn't a system call, the trip into the kernel from *Processes and System Calls*: glibc takes it with one atomic instruction and calls the kernel only when threads contend, to sleep or to wake a sleeper. Here an uncontended lock and unlock took about 9 ns and a bare system call about 100 ns, rough times from the emulated sandbox. Contention is what costs, so keep the locked part short.

example.code:
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>
#include <mutex>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    int k = 0;
    int n = 0;
    std::cin >> k >> n;
    std::mutex m;
    int64_t per_fill = 0;  // locked on every fill
    int64_t batched = 0;   // locked once per thread

    std::vector<std::thread> strategies;
    for (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) {
        strategies.emplace_back([&m, &per_fill, &batched, i, n] {
            const int64_t lots = i + 1;  // strategy i trades i + 1 lots a fill
            for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {
                std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m);
                per_fill += lots;
            }
            int64_t mine = 0;
            for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {
                mine += lots;
            }
            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m);
            batched += mine;
        });
    }
    for (std::thread& t : strategies) {
        t.join();
    }
    std::cout << "per fill " << per_fill << '\n';
    std::cout << "batched " << batched << '\n';
    std::cout << "expected " << int64_t{n} * k * (k + 1) / 2 << '\n';
    return 0;
}

