steps.body:
Some data is only correct as a group, like a position's quantity and cost basis, which change together on every fill. Put such fields behind one mutex and lock it for every read and every write, so no thread ever sees the new quantity with the old cost. Making each field safe on its own, as the atomics in the next lesson do, isn't enough: each would be right and the pair still wrong.

The group can span two objects. Each desk has its own mutex, so desks don't wait on each other, but moving risk between desks changes both, and the firm's total must never look wrong. That takes both locks at once, and order matters. If one thread locks equities then futures while another locks futures then equities, each can end up holding one lock and waiting forever for the other: a **deadlock**. Two threads written that way froze here after about 300 of their 200,000 transfers.

`std::scoped_lock` from `<mutex>` locks several mutexes together without deadlock, in whatever order threads name them, and unlocks them all at the end of the scope. C++17 deduces its template arguments, as in `std::vector v = {1, 2};`:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

struct Desk {
    std::mutex m;
    int64_t position = 0;
};

{{code}}

int main() {
    Desk equities;
    Desk futures;
    transfer(equities, futures, 3);
    std::cout << equities.position << ' ' << futures.position << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
void transfer(Desk& from, Desk& to, int64_t qty) {
    std::scoped_lock lock(from.m, to.m);
    from.position -= qty;
    to.position += qty;
}
```

The other fix is one lock order for the whole program, such as by desk id.

For a limits table that every thread reads and one updates, `std::shared_mutex` from `<shared_mutex>` lets readers in together through `std::shared_lock` and a writer alone through `std::lock_guard`. For setup that must run once, a function-local `static` is initialized safely even when threads race to it, and `std::call_once` does the same for any function.

example.code:
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>
#include <mutex>
#include <thread>

struct Desk {
    std::mutex m;
    int64_t position = 0;
};

void transfer(Desk& from, Desk& to, int64_t qty) {
    std::scoped_lock lock(from.m, to.m);  // both mutexes, deadlock free
    from.position -= qty;
    to.position += qty;
}

int64_t firm_position(Desk& a, Desk& b) {
    std::scoped_lock lock(a.m, b.m);
    return a.position + b.position;
}

int main() {
    Desk equities;
    Desk futures;
    int n = 0;
    std::cin >> equities.position >> futures.position >> n;
    const int64_t firm = equities.position + futures.position;

    int broken = 0;
    std::thread hedge([&] {
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            transfer(equities, futures, 3);
        }
    });
    std::thread unwind([&] {
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            transfer(futures, equities, 2);
        }
    });
    std::thread audit([&] {
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            if (firm_position(equities, futures) != firm) {
                ++broken;
            }
        }
    });
    hedge.join();
    unwind.join();
    audit.join();
    std::cout << "equities " << equities.position << '\n';
    std::cout << "futures " << futures.position << '\n';
    std::cout << "broken snapshots " << broken << '\n';
    return 0;
}

