steps.body:
A feed handler thread receives ticks and a strategy thread consumes them. The hottest paths spin on a lock-free queue, the subject of *A Lock-Free SPSC Ring Buffer* later in this section, but most of a trading system, from risk to logging, should sleep while there's nothing to do instead of burning a core.

`std::condition_variable` from `<condition_variable>` lets one thread sleep until another says something changed. The waiting side holds a `std::unique_lock`, a lock that `wait` can release while the thread sleeps and take again before it returns:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

struct TickQueue {
    std::mutex m_;
    std::condition_variable ready_;
    std::queue<int> q_;
    bool closed_ = true;

    void wait_for_work() {
        {{code}}
        std::cout << (q_.empty() ? "closed" : "work") << '\n';
    }
};

int main() {
    TickQueue queue;
    queue.wait_for_work();
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(m_);
ready_.wait(lock, [this] { return !q_.empty() || closed_; });
```

`wait` with a predicate checks it first, sleeps while it's false, and checks again after every wakeup, which also covers the spurious wakeups the standard allows. `[this]` captures the object, so the lambda can read its members.

The producer changes the state under the lock, then calls `notify_one()` to wake one waiter. To finish, it sets `closed_` and calls `notify_all()`, and `pop` returns `false` once the queue is closed and empty. Keep lock order in mind here too: while you hold the queue's mutex, never call code that takes another lock.

example.code:
#include <cmath>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>
#include <mutex>
#include <queue>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>

struct Tick {
    int64_t ts_ns;
    double bid, ask;
    double bid_sz, ask_sz;
};

class TickQueue {
public:
    void push(const Tick& t) {
        {
            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m_);
            q_.push(t);
        }
        ready_.notify_one();
    }

    void close() {
        {
            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m_);
            closed_ = true;
        }
        ready_.notify_all();
    }

    bool pop(Tick& out) {  // false once closed and drained
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(m_);
        ready_.wait(lock, [this] { return !q_.empty() || closed_; });
        if (q_.empty()) {
            return false;
        }
        out = q_.front();
        q_.pop();
        return true;
    }

private:
    std::mutex m_;
    std::condition_variable ready_;
    std::queue<Tick> q_;
    bool closed_ = false;
};

int main() {
    int n = 0;
    std::cin >> n;
    std::vector<Tick> feed(n);
    for (Tick& t : feed) {
        std::cin >> t.ts_ns >> t.bid >> t.ask >> t.bid_sz >> t.ask_sz;
    }

    TickQueue queue;
    std::thread feed_handler([&] {
        for (const Tick& t : feed) {
            queue.push(t);
        }
        queue.close();
    });

    int64_t count = 0;  // main is the strategy thread
    int64_t spread_ticks = 0;
    Tick t{};
    while (queue.pop(t)) {
        ++count;
        spread_ticks += std::llround((t.ask - t.bid) / 0.01);
    }
    feed_handler.join();
    std::cout << "ticks " << count << '\n';
    std::cout << "spread ticks " << spread_ticks << '\n';
    return 0;
}

